; coverage for `pick`'s parsing (reuses `let`'s own `unpack_condition` to extract the new
; constant, see eval_keyword_expression's `pick` branch): malformed `pick` usage should get a
; specific, helpful error rather than the generic "takes a new constant..." message whenever
; the specific mistake is identifiable.
load logic
bool P
arity P 1
var x
const f
use exists x (P x)

; already-declared constant instead of a fresh one
expect "EvalError"
    pick f with P f

; an extra condition on the witness is rejected (soundness: an existential witness can't
; carry an unproven extra fact with no justification, unlike `let x>0`'s universally
; quantified assumption)
expect "EvalError"
    pick a>0 with P a

; a normal, unconstrained `pick` still works fine
pick a with P a
    true
;;; Proof checked.
